 "captions": [
  {
   "start": 6.0,
   "end": 8.01,
   "lines": [
    "Whoever wrote this letter wanted it burnt,"
   ]
  },
  {
   "start": 8.26,
   "end": 10.99,
   "lines": [
    "yet four hundred years",
    "later you can still read it."
   ]
  },
  {
   "start": 11.22,
   "end": 13.61,
   "lines": [
    "There’s no name at the",
    "bottom or date at the top,"
   ]
  },
  {
   "start": 13.82,
   "end": 15.707,
   "lines": [
    "but the man who opened it knew exactly"
   ]
  },
  {
   "start": 15.74,
   "end": 18.15,
   "lines": [
    "which appointment he was",
    "being warned to miss."
   ]
  },
  {
   "start": 18.58,
   "end": 19.89,
   "lines": [
    "He was due in Parliament."
   ]
  },
  {
   "start": 20.5,
   "end": 23.95,
   "lines": [
    "Lord Monteagle could have made",
    "his excuses and stayed at home."
   ]
  },
  {
   "start": 24.32,
   "end": 28.11,
   "lines": [
    "The stranger who sent the warning",
    "would have had no reason to complain."
   ]
  },
  {
   "start": 28.46,
   "end": 31.57,
   "lines": [
    "Instead, Monteagle took the",
    "letter to a government minister."
   ]
  },
  {
   "start": 32.08,
   "end": 32.93,
   "lines": [
    "Within days,"
   ]
  },
  {
   "start": 33.24,
   "end": 37.87,
   "lines": [
    "officials at Westminster had arrested",
    "a man who called himself John Johnson."
   ]
  },
  {
   "start": 38.34,
   "end": 40.39,
   "lines": [
    "We know him as Guy Fawkes."
   ]
  },
  {
   "start": 51.529,
   "end": 54.359,
   "lines": [
    "Two years earlier, England",
    "had been waiting for a king."
   ]
  },
  {
   "start": 54.969,
   "end": 56.339,
   "lines": [
    "Elizabeth the First had died,"
   ]
  },
  {
   "start": 56.589,
   "end": 60.419,
   "lines": [
    "and the man who inherited her throne",
    "was already a king in Scotland."
   ]
  },
  {
   "start": 60.929,
   "end": 63.356,
   "lines": [
    "The Scots knew him as James the Sixth,"
   ]
  },
  {
   "start": 63.389,
   "end": 66.893,
   "lines": [
    "and now the English would",
    "know him as James the First."
   ]
  },
  {
   "start": 66.929,
   "end": 69.256,
   "lines": [
    "One man now wore both crowns."
   ]
  },
  {
   "start": 69.289,
   "end": 73.119,
   "lines": [
    "Among the people watching to see what",
    "he would do were England’s Catholics."
   ]
  },
  {
   "start": 75.61,
   "end": 79.339,
   "lines": [
    "English law required attendance at",
    "services of the established Church."
   ]
  },
  {
   "start": 79.929,
   "end": 82.139,
   "lines": [
    "Catholics who stayed",
    "away could face fines."
   ]
  },
  {
   "start": 82.609,
   "end": 84.396,
   "lines": [
    "What happened on a Sunday morning could"
   ]
  },
  {
   "start": 84.429,
   "end": 86.839,
   "lines": [
    "reach into a family’s finances for years."
   ]
  },
  {
   "start": 87.369,
   "end": 90.579,
   "lines": [
    "Some Catholics hoped a change",
    "of monarch might bring relief."
   ]
  },
  {
   "start": 91.149,
   "end": 94.359,
   "lines": [
    "James had a Catholic mother,",
    "Mary, Queen of Scots,"
   ]
  },
  {
   "start": 94.729,
   "end": 97.439,
   "lines": [
    "although his own upbringing",
    "and faith were Protestant."
   ]
  },
  {
   "start": 97.889,
   "end": 101.559,
   "lines": [
    "Then came peace between England",
    "and Spain, a Catholic power."
   ]
  },
  {
   "start": 101.829,
   "end": 105.719,
   "lines": [
    "For people hoping that peace abroad",
    "might improve their position at home,"
   ]
  },
  {
   "start": 105.989,
   "end": 107.799,
   "lines": [
    "the settlement brought disappointment."
   ]
  },
  {
   "start": 108.689,
   "end": 111.299,
   "lines": [
    "Catholics had to decide how",
    "to live under this king."
   ]
  },
  {
   "start": 111.649,
   "end": 114.559,
   "lines": [
    "One small circle of men",
    "decided he should die."
   ]
  },
  {
   "start": 114.969,
   "end": 118.419,
   "lines": [
    "Another Catholic would carry the",
    "warning that helped save his life."
   ]
  },
  {
   "start": 130.5,
   "end": 133.555,
   "lines": [
    "The man at the centre of the",
    "conspiracy was Robert Catesby,"
   ]
  },
  {
   "start": 133.765,
   "end": 135.235,
   "lines": [
    "a gentleman from Warwickshire."
   ]
  },
  {
   "start": 135.585,
   "end": 139.855,
   "lines": [
    "In May 1604, Catesby met four",
    "men in London: Thomas Winter,"
   ]
  },
  {
   "start": 140.165,
   "end": 143.272,
   "lines": [
    "Thomas Percy, John Wright and Guy Fawkes."
   ]
  },
  {
   "start": 143.305,
   "end": 144.675,
   "lines": [
    "Of those five names,"
   ]
  },
  {
   "start": 145.005,
   "end": 146.872,
   "lines": [
    "the one familiar to most people today"
   ]
  },
  {
   "start": 146.905,
   "end": 149.335,
   "lines": [
    "belongs to the man",
    "arrested at Westminster."
   ]
  },
  {
   "start": 149.985,
   "end": 152.195,
   "lines": [
    "It was Catesby who proposed the attack."
   ]
  },
  {
   "start": 156.005,
   "end": 158.272,
   "lines": [
    "His intended victims were the king and the"
   ]
  },
  {
   "start": 158.305,
   "end": 160.855,
   "lines": [
    "people assembled for the",
    "State Opening of Parliament."
   ]
  },
  {
   "start": 161.425,
   "end": 163.195,
   "lines": [
    "Consider what that occasion meant."
   ]
  },
  {
   "start": 163.665,
   "end": 166.795,
   "lines": [
    "James would address the political",
    "nation gathered around him."
   ]
  },
  {
   "start": 167.405,
   "end": 169.555,
   "lines": [
    "Members of the Commons",
    "would come to hear him."
   ]
  },
  {
   "start": 169.925,
   "end": 172.895,
   "lines": [
    "Men who disagreed about",
    "religion, about government,"
   ]
  },
  {
   "start": 173.225,
   "end": 174.652,
   "lines": [
    "even about the king himself,"
   ]
  },
  {
   "start": 174.685,
   "end": 178.115,
   "lines": [
    "would be there in the ordinary",
    "course of their duties."
   ]
  },
  {
   "start": 183.125,
   "end": 185.575,
   "lines": [
    "Catesby’s ambitions",
    "reached beyond Westminster."
   ]
  },
  {
   "start": 186.225,
   "end": 188.392,
   "lines": [
    "The conspirators hoped",
    "that Catholic gentlemen"
   ]
  },
  {
   "start": 188.425,
   "end": 190.715,
   "lines": [
    "in the Midlands would rally to them."
   ]
  },
  {
   "start": 190.925,
   "end": 194.455,
   "lines": [
    "They meant to take possession of",
    "James’s young daughter Elizabeth."
   ]
  },
  {
   "start": 194.885,
   "end": 195.685,
   "lines": [
    "On paper,"
   ]
  },
  {
   "start": 195.985,
   "end": 200.035,
   "lines": [
    "a royal child gave their imagined",
    "new government a claim to the throne."
   ]
  },
  {
   "start": 200.445,
   "end": 204.235,
   "lines": [
    "In life, she was a child whose",
    "father they intended to murder."
   ]
  },
  {
   "start": 207.483,
   "end": 209.852,
   "lines": [
    "And the support on which",
    "the whole enterprise"
   ]
  },
  {
   "start": 209.885,
   "end": 214.315,
   "lines": [
    "depended existed largely in",
    "the conspirators’ expectations."
   ]
  },
  {
   "start": 224.998,
   "end": 227.668,
   "lines": [
    "On the twenty-sixth of October, 1605,"
   ]
  },
  {
   "start": 228.118,
   "end": 231.148,
   "lines": [
    "Lord Monteagle received the",
    "warning at his house in Hoxton,"
   ]
  },
  {
   "start": 231.598,
   "end": 232.888,
   "lines": [
    "north of the City of London."
   ]
  },
  {
   "start": 233.298,
   "end": 237.608,
   "lines": [
    "A contemporary account says an unknown",
    "person delivered it to his footman."
   ]
  },
  {
   "start": 237.898,
   "end": 241.108,
   "lines": [
    "Here was a message important",
    "enough to pass to a lord,"
   ]
  },
  {
   "start": 241.438,
   "end": 243.848,
   "lines": [
    "written by someone unwilling to sign it."
   ]
  },
  {
   "start": 243.998,
   "end": 247.728,
   "lines": [
    "The writer appeals to Monteagle’s",
    "concern for his own life."
   ]
  },
  {
   "start": 251.004,
   "end": 254.848,
   "lines": [
    "He’s urged to find an excuse to miss",
    "Parliament and wait in the country."
   ]
  },
  {
   "start": 255.258,
   "end": 258.048,
   "lines": [
    "Then comes the phrase that",
    "still catches the eye."
   ]
  },
  {
   "start": 258.778,
   "end": 262.208,
   "lines": [
    "Parliament, the letter says,",
    "will receive “a terrible blow”."
   ]
  },
  {
   "start": 262.658,
   "end": 264.605,
   "lines": [
    "It gives Monteagle enough to frighten him,"
   ]
  },
  {
   "start": 264.638,
   "end": 267.448,
   "lines": [
    "and very little he could",
    "use to question the writer."
   ]
  },
  {
   "start": 268.018,
   "end": 270.848,
   "lines": [
    "Today the sheet is held",
    "by The National Archives."
   ]
  },
  {
   "start": 273.306,
   "end": 275.585,
   "lines": [
    "We can inspect the",
    "handwriting and compare the"
   ]
  },
  {
   "start": 275.618,
   "end": 277.768,
   "lines": [
    "text with accounts of what happened next."
   ]
  },
  {
   "start": 278.018,
   "end": 280.725,
   "lines": [
    "We still cannot put a name",
    "to the hand that wrote it."
   ]
  },
  {
   "start": 280.758,
   "end": 285.148,
   "lines": [
    "Francis Tresham, a conspirator",
    "and Monteagle’s brother-in-law,"
   ]
  },
  {
   "start": 285.278,
   "end": 287.288,
   "lines": [
    "came under suspicion at the time."
   ]
  },
  {
   "start": 287.758,
   "end": 291.888,
   "lines": [
    "A family connection might explain why",
    "somebody wanted to save Monteagle."
   ]
  },
  {
   "start": 292.258,
   "end": 294.568,
   "lines": [
    "It cannot establish who sent the letter."
   ]
  },
  {
   "start": 299.813,
   "end": 303.068,
   "lines": [
    "Monteagle took it to Robert",
    "Cecil, Earl of Salisbury,"
   ]
  },
  {
   "start": 303.398,
   "end": 304.808,
   "lines": [
    "the king’s chief minister."
   ]
  },
  {
   "start": 305.238,
   "end": 308.988,
   "lines": [
    "A warning addressed to one man",
    "was now in government hands."
   ]
  },
  {
   "start": 322.008,
   "end": 323.958,
   "lines": [
    "The king was away when the letter arrived."
   ]
  },
  {
   "start": 324.188,
   "end": 326.818,
   "lines": [
    "When he returned to London,",
    "he was told about it."
   ]
  },
  {
   "start": 327.108,
   "end": 330.338,
   "lines": [
    "Meanwhile, news of the warning",
    "had reached the conspirators."
   ]
  },
  {
   "start": 330.948,
   "end": 333.038,
   "lines": [
    "Catesby and Winter challenged Tresham."
   ]
  },
  {
   "start": 333.288,
   "end": 335.578,
   "lines": [
    "He persuaded them he",
    "hadn’t informed on them,"
   ]
  },
  {
   "start": 335.728,
   "end": 338.778,
   "lines": [
    "and on the third of November",
    "they decided to go on."
   ]
  },
  {
   "start": 339.088,
   "end": 339.888,
   "lines": [
    "By then,"
   ]
  },
  {
   "start": 339.988,
   "end": 344.258,
   "lines": [
    "the decision over whether Parliament",
    "could meet safely lay in other hands."
   ]
  },
  {
   "start": 348.509,
   "end": 351.658,
   "lines": [
    "On the night before the opening,",
    "officials searched at Westminster."
   ]
  },
  {
   "start": 352.308,
   "end": 356.138,
   "lines": [
    "Sir Thomas Knyvett’s party found",
    "gunpowder and took a man into custody."
   ]
  },
  {
   "start": 356.528,
   "end": 359.278,
   "lines": [
    "The king, the Lords and",
    "the Commons survived."
   ]
  },
  {
   "start": 359.628,
   "end": 363.075,
   "lines": [
    "For a moment, put aside the",
    "familiar pictures of Guy Fawkes"
   ]
  },
  {
   "start": 363.108,
   "end": 365.698,
   "lines": [
    "and look at what an",
    "official actually wrote."
   ]
  },
  {
   "start": 366.008,
   "end": 369.418,
   "lines": [
    "The House of Commons met briefly",
    "on the fifth of November."
   ]
  },
  {
   "start": 377.74,
   "end": 381.258,
   "lines": [
    "Its clerk, Ralph Ewens, made a",
    "note in the margin of the Journal."
   ]
  },
  {
   "start": 381.548,
   "end": 385.238,
   "lines": [
    "The prisoner appears there as",
    "Johnson, a servant of Thomas Percy."
   ]
  },
  {
   "start": 385.588,
   "end": 387.718,
   "lines": [
    "If that single page were all we had,"
   ]
  },
  {
   "start": 387.908,
   "end": 391.658,
   "lines": [
    "the name children learn on Bonfire",
    "Night would be missing from the story."
   ]
  },
  {
   "start": 391.928,
   "end": 394.698,
   "lines": [
    "That detail tells us something",
    "about the speed of events."
   ]
  },
  {
   "start": 395.128,
   "end": 397.975,
   "lines": [
    "The clerk could record the",
    "discovery and the danger"
   ]
  },
  {
   "start": 398.008,
   "end": 401.378,
   "lines": [
    "while the identity of the",
    "prisoner was still emerging."
   ]
  },
  {
   "start": 405.64,
   "end": 409.138,
   "lines": [
    "We know who Johnson was",
    "because other records survive."
   ]
  },
  {
   "start": 420.483,
   "end": 422.853,
   "lines": [
    "In London, investigators wanted names."
   ]
  },
  {
   "start": 423.243,
   "end": 427.673,
   "lines": [
    "In the Midlands, Catesby had to explain",
    "to potential supporters what had happened."
   ]
  },
  {
   "start": 428.107,
   "end": 431.194,
   "lines": [
    "The king was alive and",
    "the conspiracy was public."
   ]
  },
  {
   "start": 431.41,
   "end": 434.03,
   "lines": [
    "What had sounded to the",
    "plotters like the beginning of a"
   ]
  },
  {
   "start": 434.063,
   "end": 438.133,
   "lines": [
    "rising now looked very different",
    "to anyone asked to join it."
   ]
  },
  {
   "start": 438.463,
   "end": 439.833,
   "lines": [
    "On the seventh of November,"
   ]
  },
  {
   "start": 440.083,
   "end": 443.233,
   "lines": [
    "a royal proclamation",
    "declared the rebels traitors."
   ]
  },
  {
   "start": 449.561,
   "end": 451.173,
   "lines": [
    "That day, at the Tower of London,"
   ]
  },
  {
   "start": 451.503,
   "end": 453.873,
   "lines": [
    "Fawkes made the first of",
    "a series of confessions."
   ]
  },
  {
   "start": 454.443,
   "end": 456.33,
   "lines": [
    "These documents are central to the story,"
   ]
  },
  {
   "start": 456.363,
   "end": 459.533,
   "lines": [
    "and the circumstances in which",
    "they were obtained matter."
   ]
  },
  {
   "start": 460.183,
   "end": 461.213,
   "lines": [
    "Fawkes was tortured."
   ]
  },
  {
   "start": 461.603,
   "end": 463.67,
   "lines": [
    "A statement taken from",
    "a prisoner under such"
   ]
  },
  {
   "start": 463.703,
   "end": 467.073,
   "lines": [
    "pressure has to be read",
    "alongside the other evidence."
   ]
  },
  {
   "start": 467.483,
   "end": 469.37,
   "lines": [
    "By the eighth of November the flight"
   ]
  },
  {
   "start": 469.403,
   "end": 472.233,
   "lines": [
    "had ended at Holbeach",
    "House in Staffordshire."
   ]
  },
  {
   "start": 477.583,
   "end": 481.153,
   "lines": [
    "Catesby, Percy and the Wright brothers",
    "died when local forces attacked."
   ]
  },
  {
   "start": 481.783,
   "end": 482.613,
   "lines": [
    "Others were captured."
   ]
  },
  {
   "start": 482.983,
   "end": 486.553,
   "lines": [
    "There would be no trial at which",
    "Catesby, the originator of the plot,"
   ]
  },
  {
   "start": 486.763,
   "end": 488.133,
   "lines": [
    "could be questioned in public."
   ]
  },
  {
   "start": 488.603,
   "end": 492.593,
   "lines": [
    "The man who lived to be questioned,",
    "tried and condemned was Fawkes."
   ]
  },
  {
   "start": 492.983,
   "end": 495.47,
   "lines": [
    "Their different fates help",
    "explain why we have come to"
   ]
  },
  {
   "start": 495.503,
   "end": 498.113,
   "lines": [
    "know one face so much",
    "better than the others."
   ]
  },
  {
   "start": 510.5,
   "end": 513.252,
   "lines": [
    "On the twenty-seventh of January, 1606,"
   ]
  },
  {
   "start": 513.582,
   "end": 516.332,
   "lines": [
    "eight conspirators went on",
    "trial at Westminster Hall."
   ]
  },
  {
   "start": 516.822,
   "end": 520.236,
   "lines": [
    "The executions followed on the",
    "thirtieth and thirty-first."
   ]
  },
  {
   "start": 520.4,
   "end": 524.272,
   "lines": [
    "Fawkes died on the second day, in",
    "Old Palace Yard at Westminster."
   ]
  },
  {
   "start": 524.982,
   "end": 527.269,
   "lines": [
    "Less than three months",
    "had passed since the"
   ]
  },
  {
   "start": 527.302,
   "end": 530.792,
   "lines": [
    "morning his assumed name",
    "appeared in the Commons Journal."
   ]
  },
  {
   "start": 535.636,
   "end": 538.712,
   "lines": [
    "For English Catholics who had",
    "played no part in the conspiracy,"
   ]
  },
  {
   "start": 539.062,
   "end": 541.052,
   "lines": [
    "its consequences were only beginning."
   ]
  },
  {
   "start": 541.442,
   "end": 542.892,
   "lines": [
    "When Parliament reassembled,"
   ]
  },
  {
   "start": 543.282,
   "end": 546.452,
   "lines": [
    "some Protestant members demanded",
    "action against Catholics."
   ]
  },
  {
   "start": 547.082,
   "end": 549.832,
   "lines": [
    "James himself acknowledged",
    "that most were loyal."
   ]
  },
  {
   "start": 549.922,
   "end": 553.469,
   "lines": [
    "Yet he supported an oath requiring",
    "Catholics to affirm their"
   ]
  },
  {
   "start": 553.502,
   "end": 557.732,
   "lines": [
    "allegiance to him and reject the",
    "Pope’s authority to depose him."
   ]
  },
  {
   "start": 558.062,
   "end": 559.212,
   "lines": [
    "Think back to the letter."
   ]
  },
  {
   "start": 562.468,
   "end": 565.852,
   "lines": [
    "Monteagle had been faced with a",
    "threat to people of different beliefs."
   ]
  },
  {
   "start": 566.222,
   "end": 569.749,
   "lines": [
    "He had taken it to the authorities",
    "while there was still time to act."
   ]
  },
  {
   "start": 569.782,
   "end": 574.044,
   "lines": [
    "If you were looking for",
    "evidence of what a Catholic"
   ]
  },
  {
   "start": 574.084,
   "end": 575.309,
   "lines": [
    "subject might do when",
    "the king was in danger,"
   ]
  },
  {
   "start": 575.342,
   "end": 579.512,
   "lines": [
    "his conduct was there in the official",
    "account of the king’s deliverance."
   ]
  },
  {
   "start": 579.802,
   "end": 581.092,
   "lines": [
    "For his fellow Catholics,"
   ]
  },
  {
   "start": 581.422,
   "end": 584.852,
   "lines": [
    "that example offered little",
    "protection against suspicion."
   ]
  },
  {
   "start": 587.728,
   "end": 589.892,
   "lines": [
    "A handful of men had chosen treason."
   ]
  },
  {
   "start": 590.342,
   "end": 593.852,
   "lines": [
    "People who had never met them would",
    "be asked to prove their loyalty."
   ]
  },
  {
   "start": 594.362,
   "end": 597.089,
   "lines": [
    "The arrests ended the",
    "immediate peril to Parliament."
   ]
  },
  {
   "start": 597.122,
   "end": 602.552,
   "lines": [
    "They could do little to undo what the",
    "conspiracy had set in motion outside it."
   ]
  },
  {
   "start": 610.09,
   "end": 611.6,
   "lines": [
    "In January 1606,"
   ]
  },
  {
   "start": 611.99,
   "end": 615.54,
   "lines": [
    "Parliament also decided how the fifth",
    "of November should be remembered."
   ]
  },
  {
   "start": 615.99,
   "end": 618.82,
   "lines": [
    "It passed a law providing",
    "for an annual thanksgiving."
   ]
  },
  {
   "start": 619.23,
   "end": 620.03,
   "lines": [
    "Each year,"
   ]
  },
  {
   "start": 620.17,
   "end": 624.32,
   "lines": [
    "congregations would hear again how the",
    "king and Parliament had been delivered."
   ]
  },
  {
   "start": 624.79,
   "end": 627.62,
   "lines": [
    "Before the anniversary",
    "became a familiar night out,"
   ]
  },
  {
   "start": 627.75,
   "end": 629.84,
   "lines": [
    "it was a date on the church calendar,"
   ]
  },
  {
   "start": 629.93,
   "end": 633.38,
   "lines": [
    "with a lesson that preachers",
    "were expected to draw from it."
   ]
  },
  {
   "start": 637.501,
   "end": 640.96,
   "lines": [
    "Beyond the church doors, bells",
    "rang and people lit bonfires."
   ]
  },
  {
   "start": 641.37,
   "end": 644.5,
   "lines": [
    "Some of the earliest celebrations",
    "even included fireworks."
   ]
  },
  {
   "start": 644.93,
   "end": 647.26,
   "lines": [
    "What went into the fire",
    "changed with the times."
   ]
  },
  {
   "start": 647.75,
   "end": 649.664,
   "lines": [
    "In the reign of Charles the First,"
   ]
  },
  {
   "start": 649.71,
   "end": 653.26,
   "lines": [
    "there are signs of people burning",
    "figures of the Pope or the devil."
   ]
  },
  {
   "start": 653.65,
   "end": 658.02,
   "lines": [
    "At moments of particular anxiety about",
    "a Catholic successor to the throne,"
   ]
  },
  {
   "start": 658.37,
   "end": 660.7,
   "lines": [
    "such displays became more popular."
   ]
  },
  {
   "start": 664.932,
   "end": 667.157,
   "lines": [
    "The figure on the bonfire",
    "had a history of its"
   ]
  },
  {
   "start": 667.19,
   "end": 670.74,
   "lines": [
    "own before it usually bore",
    "the name of Guy Fawkes."
   ]
  },
  {
   "start": 671.07,
   "end": 672.877,
   "lines": [
    "By the nineteenth century there were"
   ]
  },
  {
   "start": 672.91,
   "end": 676.3,
   "lines": [
    "chants associated with",
    "collecting money and firewood."
   ]
  },
  {
   "start": 676.83,
   "end": 679.957,
   "lines": [
    "Children could take part in",
    "the ritual without ever having"
   ]
  },
  {
   "start": 679.99,
   "end": 683.543,
   "lines": [
    "heard the names of Robert",
    "Catesby or Ralph Ewens."
   ]
  },
  {
   "start": 685.453,
   "end": 686.52,
   "lines": [
    "Across generations,"
   ]
  },
  {
   "start": 686.85,
   "end": 689.237,
   "lines": [
    "a complicated political story had acquired"
   ]
  },
  {
   "start": 689.27,
   "end": 691.66,
   "lines": [
    "something almost any child could remember."
   ]
  },
  {
   "start": 692.03,
   "end": 695.457,
   "lines": [
    "Guy Fawkes, the fifth of",
    "November and a fire in the dark."
   ]
  },
  {
   "start": 695.49,
   "end": 699.04,
   "lines": [
    "Back in the archive, the",
    "letter is quieter company."
   ]
  },
  {
   "start": 699.41,
   "end": 703.56,
   "lines": [
    "Its writer left no signature and",
    "asked that the page be destroyed."
   ]
  },
  {
   "start": 704.17,
   "end": 705.3,
   "lines": [
    "Monteagle kept it."
   ]
  },
  {
   "start": 705.43,
   "end": 708.48,
   "lines": [
    "Of all the objects that might",
    "stand for the Gunpowder Plot,"
   ]
  },
  {
   "start": 708.87,
   "end": 712.12,
   "lines": [
    "perhaps the most telling is",
    "this fragile sheet of paper,"
   ]
  },
  {
   "start": 712.37,
   "end": 715.5,
   "lines": [
    "still here because somebody",
    "chose to pass it on."
   ]
  }
 ]
}
